{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789832184,"nonce":"6q/pC0LA/Sj6veKFfoTWFfWhVeg=","title":"Null\u003cInt64\u003e holds the raw i64 and reads it as a pointer","message":"TestInt64.testGen: `var n:Null\u003cInt64\u003e = null; n = Int64.make(0xf0f0f0f0, 0xefefefef); eq(n.high, ...)` SIGSEGVs at fault address 0xf0f0f0f0efefeff7 -- the value plus a field offset. Int64 is the native i64, so `Null\u003cInt64\u003e` needs the box protocol the way `Null\u003cInt\u003e` has it (haxe_box_int_ptr carries a 64-bit payload already): box on the store, unbox before a word read, an arithmetic op or an Int64 parameter, `== null` through haxe_dynamic_is_null. Issue4842 is the same cluster.","files":null}]}